# Summary
Introduced: the new debug snapshot fields collect payload reclaim root object keys and the debug body prints those keys over the unauthenticated local debug endpoint.
When ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT is enabled, requests to /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/<bucket> are handled before normal S3 authentication. This commit adds payload reclaim roots to that response and formats root.key directly into the HTTP body. Object keys can be sensitive metadata, and the existing ServeConfig documentation explicitly says these local debug endpoints do not return keys. The binary configuration limits this endpoint to loopback listeners and it is disabled by default, so the practical exposure is limited to deployments that intentionally enable the local debug endpoint and to local users/processes or any proxy that exposes the loopback service. Still, this is a security regression because an unauthenticated caller can learn private object key names for reclaim-blocked objects in a bucket.

# Resolution
Fixed for production/release builds by the local-debug endpoint hardening commits:

- `45497afe` gates local debug route dispatch and helper code behind `cfg(any(test, feature = "local-debug-endpoints"))`, and rejects `ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=1` at startup unless the binary was compiled with that non-production support.
- `73a36bcd` makes `local-debug-endpoints` a debug/test-only feature by adding a release-build `compile_error!`, and updates UAT smoke handling so debug-hook smokes cannot be run against release or externally supplied binaries.

The payload-reclaim-root debug output remains available in explicit debug/test builds for diagnostics, but release artifacts cannot include or enable the unauthenticated debug route. Production default builds reject the environment variable before startup.

