# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Kept at low. The finding is valid and in the main runtime: unauthenticated local debug handling precedes normal S3 authentication, and the debug body now prints ObjectKey values. However, probability and blast radius are limited by explicit controls: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults false, argmin-s3 requires a frontend role, and config rejects non-loopback listen addresses for this mode. The impact is limited information disclosure of object key names for specific reclaim-blocked objects, with no object payload, credential, identity, or integrity compromise shown.
## Likelihood
low - Exploitation is straightforward once reachable, but the endpoint is disabled by default and the main binary enforces loopback-only binding when it is enabled. A remote attacker would need an additional proxy or deployment mistake.
## Impact
low - The disclosed data is object key metadata for payload reclaim roots, not object contents, secret keys, authentication tokens, or write/delete capability. Object keys can still be sensitive because they may encode user IDs, paths, names, or business metadata.
## Assumptions
- The main production artifact is the argmin-s3 binary from crates/argmin-s3.
- A realistic attacker for this issue is an unprivileged local user/process on the same host, or a remote user only if an operator or proxy exposes the loopback-bound service.
- Object keys may contain sensitive business or user metadata even when object payloads are not disclosed.
- No repository IaC or deployment manifest was found that publicly exposes this debug endpoint.
- ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT enabled
- argmin-s3 process role includes a frontend
- listener is reachable on loopback or exposed by a local proxy/misconfiguration
- bucket has payload reclaim roots that include object keys
## Path
Local caller -> loopback argmin-s3 -> pre-auth /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/<bucket> -> storage snapshot -> object key names in response
## Path evidence
- `crates/argmin-s3/Cargo.toml:6-8` - Declares argmin-s3 as a production binary, placing the affected config and HTTP server path in product runtime scope.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:186-192` - Documentation says local debug endpoints are operator-only and do not return keys.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:691-700` - When local_debug_endpoint is enabled, local_debug_response is returned before the normal S3 request handling/authentication path.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1041-1068` - Defines GET /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/<bucket>, parses an attacker-controlled bucket path component, and returns local_debug_bucket_delete_attempt_body.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:11781-11810` - Retrieves bucket payload reclaim roots and copies root.key into the debug snapshot data structure.
- `crates/storage/src/types.rs:2532-2537` - The debug snapshot type explicitly contains pub key: ObjectKey.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1240-1255` - The formatter emits root.key.as_str() into the plaintext HTTP response body.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:126-151` - Configuration documentation shows the default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000 and ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults false.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:322-324` - Config parsing defaults local_debug_endpoint to false unless ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT is set.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:444-460` - Mitigation: when local debug is enabled, config requires a frontend process role and rejects non-loopback ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1996-2052` - The binary binds the configured TCP listener and passes config.local_debug_endpoint into server-http ServeConfig.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:5142-5241` - Existing test constructs a snapshot with an ObjectKey and asserts the formatted body contains the key value.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:5274-5298` - Existing test demonstrates an enabled local debug endpoint accepts a raw HTTP request without S3 auth headers.
## Narrative
This is a real but low-severity security regression. The main argmin-s3 binary passes local_debug_endpoint into server-http, and the handler returns local debug responses before the normal S3 authentication path. The bucket-delete-attempt debug route accepts a bucket name and returns a text snapshot. The new storage snapshot population copies root.key into BucketDeleteDebugPayloadReclaimRoot, and the formatter writes root.key.as_str() directly into the HTTP body. That contradicts the ServeConfig documentation saying local debug endpoints do not return keys. Reachability is constrained: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults false, the binary requires a frontend role, and config rejects non-loopback listen addresses when the debug endpoint is enabled. Therefore the practical attacker is local or someone who can reach a mistakenly proxied loopback service, and the impact is disclosure of object key metadata rather than object payloads or credentials.
## Controls
- flag: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults to false
- network: argmin-s3 rejects non-loopback listen addresses when local debug is enabled
- role check: local debug requires a frontend process role
- authn gap: local debug responses are evaluated before normal S3 SigV4 authentication
- deployment: no repository IaC ingress, load balancer, or Kubernetes manifest exposing this endpoint was found
## Blindspots
- Static repository review cannot confirm how real deployments set ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT.
- No deployment manifests were present to prove whether operators proxy loopback debug paths externally.
- The exact sensitivity of object key names is application-dependent.
- The validation evidence demonstrates formatter leakage and unauthenticated debug reachability, but not a full live bucket-delete-attempt end-to-end deployment with real reclaim roots.
